# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE COMPLETE ONE-BOX LIVE ENV — copy this whole file, fill in the eight «FILL IN» values.
#
# ONE box. ONE underlying. ONE lot per leg. Four legs submitted concurrently within each hedge
# wave. Live orders. Under continuous human supervision.
#
# EVERYTHING you need is here — process, database, access gate, Zerodha credentials and the Box
# strategy. Only SECRETS and CONNECTION STRINGS are left blank, marked «FILL IN». Every other
# value is set, and every variable name has been checked to exist in the source.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE EIGHT VALUES YOU MUST PROVIDE
#   1. DATABASE_URL                    PostgreSQL — the operational authority, not optional
#   2. FRONTEND_URL                    exact origin of your UI (https in production)
#   3. SITE_ACCESS_SECRET              the site passcode; blank = gate CLOSED, nothing works
#   4. BROKER_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY     exactly 32 bytes, as 64-char hex or base64
#   5. KITE_API_KEY                    from your Kite Connect app
#   6. KITE_API_SECRET                 from your Kite Connect app
#   7. KITE_REDIRECT_URL               must match the Kite app's registered redirect EXACTLY
#   8. CALSPREAD_DEPLOYMENT_ID         lock namespace; unset means it is INFERRED (see §2)
#
# ⚠ DO NOT START IN LIVE MODE ON A FRESH DEPLOYMENT. A live boot requires an already-authenticated
#   Zerodha session, and you obtain one by logging in through the UI — which needs the backend up.
#   Start in PAPER, log in, then promote to live and restart. Full explanation and the exact
#   ordering are in §8; the day's runbook in §17 follows that order.
#
# Optional: MONGODB_URI (reporting replica only — PostgreSQL stays authoritative without it).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# AFTER APPLYING, VERIFY WHAT IS ACTUALLY IN FORCE. Values can also come from the shell, PM2's
# own env, or an ecosystem file, and this file is not the authority on the running process:
#
#     node dist/box/effectiveConfig.js
#
# ⚠ WHAT A TYPO DOES — verified per variable, because the answer differs.
#
#   REFUSE TO BOOT on a malformed value (loud, harmless): BOX_MAX_OPEN_BOXES, both
#   BOX_SESSION_MAX_* ceilings, BOX_LIVE_MAX_OPEN_LEG_QUANTITY, BOX_LIVE_MAX_GROSS_OPEN_LEG_
#   QUANTITY, BOX_LIVE_MAX_BOX_CAPITAL_RUPEES, BOX_LIVE_DAILY_LOSS_LIMIT,
#   BOX_ONE_ACTIVE_BOX_PER_UNDERLYING, BOX_ENABLE_SHORT_BOX, APP_TIMEZONE, FRONTEND_URL, PORT,
#   BROKER_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY (wrong length is rejected, not truncated).
#
#   ✔ NO LONGER SILENTLY LOOSER. These four used to fall back LOOSER than the value set here, so a
#     typo weakened the trial envelope without saying so, and the only mitigation was remembering to
#     read them back from effectiveConfig. They now REFUSE TO BOOT on an explicitly-set malformed
#     value, like the ceilings listed above:
#       BOX_LIVE_MAX_RESIDUAL_LEGS         was: typo → 1  (tolerated a residual leg; we ask 0)
#       BOX_MAX_CONCURRENT_PER_UNDERLYING  was: typo → 2  (permitted two; we ask 1)
#       BOX_MAX_UNDERLYINGS                was: typo → 0  (NO CAP AT ALL; we ask 1)
#       BOX_STRIKE_LEVEL                   was: typo → 3  (ATM ±3, a wider net; we ask 1)
#     Their `0` sentinels are UNCHANGED: BOX_MAX_UNDERLYINGS=0 still means an unbounded universe and
#     BOX_LIVE_MAX_RESIDUAL_LEGS=0 still means "tolerate none". Only malformed input became fatal.
#
#   Fall back SAFE (to off/false), so a typo cannot arm anything: BOX_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED,
#   ZERODHA_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED, BOX_SHADOW_MODE_ENABLED, and
#   BOX_LIVE_ENTRY_SUBMIT_CONCURRENCY (garbage collapses to 1, the slow safe width).
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ═══ §1. PROCESS ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
PORT=3001
NODE_ENV=production

# Must be exactly this. Every trading-day, token-scheduling and P&L-day decision in this backend
# is an IST decision, so anything else is REFUSED at boot rather than honoured.
APP_TIMEZONE=Asia/Kolkata

# «FILL IN» REQUIRED — boot fails without it. CORS is exact-origin with credentials, never a
# wildcard. Must be https when NODE_ENV=production, because the session cookie is Secure-only.
# No trailing slash.
FRONTEND_URL=

# Origin required on mutating requests. Blank = defaults to FRONTEND_URL, which is what you want
# unless the UI is served from a second origin.
CSRF_ALLOWED_ORIGIN=

SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS=20000


# ═══ §2. IDENTITY / LOCK NAMESPACE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# «FILL IN» Set this EXPLICITLY. Unset, the durable reservation lock namespace is INFERRED from
# NODE_ENV — and two environments can then share one, so a laptop could take a contract this
# process needs. The engine warns about this at boot in live mode. Use something unmistakable,
# e.g. gts-prod-azure-cin.
#
# You can see whether it took effect in the boot log:
#     [Box] durable instrument reservations READY (deployment=<this value> store=...)
# If that reads `deployment=production` you have left this blank and it was inferred from NODE_ENV.
CALSPREAD_DEPLOYMENT_ID=

# The region LABEL for execution-latency calibration samples. NEVER auto-detected — it is a label
# you choose, and the calibration store refuses to read another region's samples
# (`WHERE region = $1`, repository.ts).
#
# INERT IN THIS CONFIGURATION, and worth setting anyway. Samples are only WRITTEN when
# BOX_LIVE_TIMING_PERSIST_ENABLED=true (default false) and only READ by the paper `live_parity`
# profile (we run `standard`), so today nothing consults it. It becomes load-bearing the moment you
# enable either — and the failure mode if it is blank is silent: an unset region stores and reads
# under `region IS NULL`, so samples gathered on a PREVIOUS host would be mixed with this one's as
# though the network path were the same. Label it now and that can never happen.
#
# The value is arbitrary but must be stable and distinct per network path. On Azure Central India
# (Pune) `azure-centralindia` is clearer than a cloud-agnostic guess; the previous AWS Mumbai
# deployment would have been `ap-south-1`, and those two must not share a label.
BOX_DEPLOYMENT_REGION=azure-centralindia

# Optional per-process label; useful when you later run more than one worker.
CALSPREAD_INSTANCE_ID=


# ═══ §3. POSTGRESQL — THE OPERATIONAL AUTHORITY ══════════════════════════════════════════════
# «FILL IN» REQUIRED. Not optional: durable order intents, the trading-session row and the
# excluded-underlyings blocklist all live here. Without it the session-budget layer is INERT and
# live entry is blocked.
#
# NOTE the database NAME in the example below is `strikedge`. That is a load-bearing legacy
# identifier, not a branding oversight — do not "correct" it unless you also migrate the data.
#   e.g. postgres://user:pass@127.0.0.1:5432/strikedge
DATABASE_URL=

PG_POOL_MAX=10
PG_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
PG_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS=2000
PG_APPLICATION_NAME=gts

# Applies pending SQL migrations at boot. Migration 012 (the blocklist table) must be applied
# before the run. Leave true for a single-process deployment — the advisory lock makes a
# simultaneous second starter safe. Set false to require `npm run migrate` as a release step.
PG_MIGRATE_ON_BOOT=true


# ═══ §4. ACCESS GATE ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# «FILL IN» REQUIRED in practice. A single passcode gates the whole UI and API. If this is blank
# the gate is FAIL CLOSED — every /api/box/* request is refused, so nothing works at all.
# Use a long random string.
SITE_ACCESS_SECRET=

SITE_SESSION_TTL_HOURS=24
SESSION_COOKIE_NAME=gts_session


# ═══ §5. BROKER TOKEN STORAGE ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# «FILL IN» REQUIRED. Encrypts broker access tokens at rest in PostgreSQL. Must decode to
# EXACTLY 32 bytes — 64 hex characters, or 44-character base64. Generate with:
#     openssl rand -hex 32
BROKER_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# in_app = this deployment mints its own Kite token via the login flow (§6). This is the mode you
# want. `provider` instead polls an external token service and needs the KITE_TOKEN_BROKER_*
# values, which are deliberately omitted here.
BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=in_app

# Key rotation only — leave both blank in normal operation.
BROKER_TOKEN_OLD_KEY=
BROKER_TOKEN_NEW_KEY=


# ═══ §6. ZERODHA / KITE ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# «FILL IN» All three REQUIRED for live. From your Kite Connect app at developers.kite.trade.
KITE_API_KEY=
KITE_API_SECRET=

# «FILL IN» Must match the redirect URL registered on the Kite app EXACTLY, character for
# character, or the login exchange fails. Typically:  <FRONTEND_URL>/api/broker/zerodha/callback
KITE_REDIRECT_URL=

# Blank uses the documented defaults — https://kite.zerodha.com/connect/login and
# https://api.kite.trade. Only set these to point at a mock.
KITE_LOGIN_URL=
KITE_API_ROOT=
KITE_HTTP_TIMEOUT_MS=7000

DEFAULT_ACTIVE_BROKER=zerodha

# Keep FALSE. A silent broker switch mid-session changes which account owns the exposure, which
# is the last thing you want during a supervised single-box run.
AUTO_FALLBACK_TO_DHAN=false


# ═══ §7. MONGODB — ASYNCHRONOUS REPORTING REPLICA, OPTIONAL ══════════════════════════════════
# Leave blank and export is simply disabled; PostgreSQL remains authoritative and nothing about
# the trade path changes. An Atlas outage is a non-event by design.
MONGODB_URI=
MONGO_EXPORT_ENABLED=true
MONGO_EXPORT_BATCH_SIZE=100
MONGO_EXPORT_INTERVAL_MS=1000
MONGO_EXPORT_MAX_BACKOFF_MS=60000
MONGO_EXPORT_SERVER_SELECTION_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
MONGO_EXPORT_MAX_ATTEMPTS=10

# Blank = the db parsed from MONGODB_URI, else the `strikedge` fallback. Same legacy-identifier
# note as DATABASE_URL: leaving it blank is correct.
MONGO_EXPORT_DB=

# One-off legacy import tooling only. Leave blank.
LEGACY_BOX_MONGODB_URI=


# ═══ §8. GO LIVE — three switches, and you CANNOT start here ═════════════════════════════════
#
# ⚠⚠ READ THIS BEFORE SETTING THESE THREE. A live-mode boot REQUIRES an already-authenticated
#    Zerodha session:
#
#        engine.ts:  if (!this.deps.marketData.isAuthenticated())
#                      throw new Error("[Box] live execution blocked: the zerodha broker session
#                                       is missing.");
#
#    With BROKER_LOGIN_MODE=in_app you obtain that session by logging in through the UI — which
#    needs the backend running. So on a fresh deployment, or any morning before you have logged
#    in, setting these three to live/true/true makes the process CRASH-LOOP on boot with
#    BOOT_FAILURE. It is not a misconfiguration; it is an ordering problem.
#
#    THE ORDER THAT WORKS, and it is one extra restart:
#
#      1. Set the three values below to the PAPER posture:
#             BOX_EXECUTION_MODE=paper_legging
#             BOX_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=false
#             ZERODHA_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=false
#      2. Start the backend. It boots without a broker session.
#      3. Log in to Zerodha through the UI. The token is encrypted into PostgreSQL, so it
#         survives a restart.
#      4. Set the three values to the LIVE posture below and restart. isAuthenticated() is now
#         true and the live boot succeeds.
#
#    Step 2 doubles as the paper rehearsal, in the only order it can happen in.
#
# ⚠ THIS RECURS EVERY MORNING. A Kite session ends at the IST day boundary, and a token minted
#   yesterday is treated as STALE — so `authenticated` is false again. A live-mode backend
#   therefore cannot boot before somebody has logged in for the day, and a pm2 restart overnight
#   will crash-loop exactly as a fresh deployment does.
#
#   Recommended posture: START THE DAY IN PAPER, log in, then promote to live and restart. It
#   costs one restart and removes an entire class of morning failure. Running live-by-default is
#   only safe while a same-day token already exists.
BOX_EXECUTION_MODE=live
BOX_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=true
ZERODHA_LIVE_TRADING_ENABLED=true

# Mutually exclusive with live; restated so a copy-paste cannot re-enable them and fail at boot.
BOX_SHADOW_MODE_ENABLED=false
BOX_PAPER_EXECUTION_PROFILE=standard


# ═══ §9. ONLY ONE BOX ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BOX_MAX_OPEN_BOXES is the control that actually answers "I cannot fund a second box". It is the
# only global ceiling enforced in EVERY execution mode and decided BEFORE any exposure exists,
# and it counts committed exposure — open positions, unresolved partial entries, in-flight entry
# pipelines, confirmed-but-unrecorded fills, and reservations held over an uncertain outcome.
# At 1, a second box is refused on ANY underlying, in EITHER direction.
BOX_MAX_OPEN_BOXES=1

# Bounds what you TRY, not what you hold. An attempt that posted orders, partially filled and was
# unwound took real exposure and paid real charges, yet holds no inventory afterwards — so only
# this catches it. It is ALSO the only thing that refuses two entries admitted in the same
# instant. Do not raise it "to allow a retry": arm a fresh session instead.
BOX_SESSION_MAX_ENTRY_ATTEMPTS=1
BOX_SESSION_MAX_COMPLETED_TRADES=1

# Refuses a second box on the same underlying regardless of strike pair, expiry or direction.
# The code default is false — deliberately ON here.
BOX_ONE_ACTIVE_BOX_PER_UNDERLYING=true
BOX_MAX_CONCURRENT_PER_UNDERLYING=1

# ⚠ 0 does NOT mean unlimited here — the guard is `openBoxes >= limit`, so 0 means NO box may
#   ever open. This one is LIVE-ONLY and is read from a count refreshed only after a position
#   exists, which is exactly why BOX_MAX_OPEN_BOXES above exists.
BOX_LIVE_MAX_OPEN_BOXES=1
BOX_LIVE_MAX_CONCURRENT_EXECUTIONS=1

# ⚠ the comparison is STRICTLY GREATER, so 0 tolerates no residual leg at all.
BOX_LIVE_MAX_RESIDUAL_LEGS=0

# How long unresolved recovery may persist before operational status ESCALATES. This is an ALERT, not
# an instruction: nothing is flattened, cancelled or reversed because the timer expired. New entry is
# already refused for the underlying state (incomplete reconciliation, unknown orders, unattended
# working orders, active recovery, the crash-recovery quarantine, residual legs over the limit) — the
# escalation adds the distinction between "recovery is in progress" and "recovery has been stuck
# longer than you said was acceptable", with the age and bounded counts, under the reason
# `recovery_escalation_timeout`.
#
# Exits, protective cancels, emergency residual flattening, reconciliation and broker-state refresh
# all continue at and after escalation.
#
# For RESIDUAL exposure the age comes from the leg's durable `created_at`, so it SURVIVES A RESTART.
# For the other unresolved states no durable timestamp exists, so their age is measured from when the
# process first observed them and RESETS on restart — the status reports which source is in play, so
# the weaker one is never mistaken for the stronger. 0 disables escalation only.
BOX_LIVE_RECOVERY_ESCALATION_MS=120000


# ═══ §10. THE FOUR-LEG BURST — DELIBERATELY SERIAL FOR THE FIRST RUN ═════════════════════════
# 1 = one order in flight at a time, for the whole entry. The burst architecture is untouched and
# still supports 2-4; this profile just does not use it yet.
#
# WHY SERIAL FIRST. Concurrency was never a safety problem here — entry is hedge-first in two
# waves (2 BUYs, then the 2 uncovered SELLs held until the hedges have attributed fill proof), and
# the scheduler cannot overlap a SELL with its own hedge at any width. What concurrency does change
# is how READABLE the first real run is. At 1 the broker's order log, the backend's timeline and
# the operator's screen all show the same four events in the same order, so "which order was in
# flight when that happened" is never a question you have to reconstruct. That is worth more on a
# first live test than the ~110 ms per wave that width would save.
#
# The gap between placements is a real rate-limit wait either way — Zerodha 110 ms, hard floor
# 100 ms — so serial costs roughly one extra pacing interval per wave, not a multiple.
#
# Raise to 2 only after a clean supervised run, and change ONE thing at a time.
BOX_LIVE_ENTRY_SUBMIT_CONCURRENCY=1
BOX_LIVE_BROKER_ORDER_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=0
BOX_LIVE_BROKER_MIN_INTERVAL_MS=250


# ═══ §11. THE UNIVERSE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BOX_MAX_UNDERLYINGS caps HOW MANY names are considered, chosen by the board's own priority
# order (indices first, then alphabetical) — it caps COUNT, not IDENTITY, so on its own it does
# not guarantee the one name you get is the one you intended. Pair it with the live allowlist in
# §11b, which does constrain identity. At 1 you get NIFTY, which is why the quantity ceilings in
# §12 are sized for NIFTY's lot — a number that CHANGES, so see the warning there.
#
# To choose BY NAME, use the operator blocklist — runtime, no restart:
#     GET    /api/box/excluded-underlyings
#     POST   /api/box/excluded-underlyings   {"symbol":"NYKAA","reason":"illiquid strikes"}
#     DELETE /api/box/excluded-underlyings/NYKAA
BOX_MAX_UNDERLYINGS=1
BOX_STRIKE_LEVEL=1
BOX_ENABLE_SHORT_BOX=false

# ═══ §11b. THE LIVE ENTRY ALLOWLIST — IDENTITY, WHICH THE COUNT CAP CANNOT GIVE YOU ═══════════
# BOX_MAX_UNDERLYINGS=1 caps HOW MANY names are considered; it does not say WHICH. The one that
# survives is chosen by the board's own priority order, so a universe change, an index reshuffle or
# a newly-listed name can move which instrument this trial actually trades — without any setting in
# this file changing. That is precisely the kind of silent substitution a supervised first run must
# not be exposed to.
#
# This list constrains identity. A name not on it is refused for NEW ENTRY at the cheap scanner
# layer AND at the authoritative lower layers, because the allowlist is composed into the same
# verdict function the operator blocklist already uses — so it cannot be bypassed by anything that
# skips the scanner.
#
# ⚠ EMPTY OR UNSET MEANS UNCONSTRAINED, not "nothing may trade". Unset is how every deployment
#   before this feature is configured, and reading it as a total halt would turn an upgrade into an
#   outage. Set it explicitly, as here.
#
# It is ENTRY-ONLY. Removing a name mid-session does not strand a position: exits, reductions,
# protective cancels and reconciliation are all unaffected for exposure already owned.
#
# Comma-separated; symbols are normalised (trimmed, upper-cased, de-duplicated) with the same rules
# as the blocklist, so "nifty" and " NIFTY " are the same entry.
BOX_LIVE_ALLOWED_UNDERLYINGS=NIFTY


# ═══ §11c. «EDIT» ZERODHA STATIC IP — A REAL BROKER REQUIREMENT, AND A LOCAL POLICY GATE ══════
# TWO DIFFERENT THINGS. Keep them apart or you will trust the wrong one.
#
#   1. THE BROKER REQUIREMENT (outside GAR_B). Zerodha requires the public egress IP that sends API
#      order requests to be registered in the Kite developer console. Requests from an unregistered
#      address may be REJECTED by Zerodha. Register the address before the run:
#          Kite developer console → your app → add the server's static public IP
#      Confirm the host actually egresses from it, e.g.  curl -s https://api.ipify.org
#      (run that by hand during preflight — GAR_B deliberately performs no such lookup itself, so
#      the order path never acquires an external dependency).
#
#   2. GAR_B'S POLICY GATE (below). ZERODHA_STATIC_IP_CONFIRMED records that YOU did step 1. It is
#      an OPERATOR CONFIRMATION and nothing more. GAR_B cannot verify the whitelist: Kite exposes no
#      endpoint that answers "is this IP registered for my API key", so a confirmed flag is never
#      reported as broker-verified. A matching egress IP would not prove it either.
#
# ⚠ FAILS CLOSED FOR NEW ENTRY. Unset, blank, false or a typo ("ture") all read as NOT confirmed, and
#   a live Zerodha deployment then refuses NEW ENTRY with the reason `zerodha_static_ip_unconfirmed`,
#   visible in operational_readiness.entry.reasons.
#
# ⚠ IT DOES NOT GATE REDUCTION, DELIBERATELY. Exits, protective cancels, emergency residual
#   flattening and reconciliation are still ATTEMPTED with the flag unset. A forgotten checkbox must
#   never become a reason to leave real exposure unmanaged. If the host genuinely is on an
#   unregistered address, Zerodha may reject those requests itself — that surfaces as a broker /
#   infrastructure failure to escalate, which is the honest place for it.
ZERODHA_STATIC_IP_CONFIRMED=true
ZERODHA_EXPECTED_EGRESS_IP=CHANGEME_STATIC_PUBLIC_IP   # e.g. 203.0.113.7 — diagnostics only


# ═══ §12. «EDIT» QUANTITY AND CAPITAL — SIZED FOR NIFTY (LOT 65 AT TIME OF WRITING) ══════════
# ⚠ THESE WILL REFUSE A SINGLE-STOCK BOX AS WRITTEN. That is deliberate, and a test pins it. A
#   stock lot is far larger — PAYTM is 725 — so a per-leg cap of 65 refuses the entry before the
#   first order leaves. It looks exactly like "nothing ever qualifies".
#
# ⚠ THIS FILE IS NOT THE AUTHORITY ON LOT SIZE. THE INSTRUMENT MASTER IS.
#   The order quantity actually sent is always `candidate.lot_size` read from the live instrument
#   master — never a number from this file. These two caps are a CONTAINMENT CHECK on that
#   quantity, so they have to agree with it, and a stale number here does not shrink the order:
#   it either refuses every entry (cap below one lot) or silently permits more than one lot's
#   worth of exposure (cap above it).
#
#   That is not hypothetical. This file previously shipped 75/300 with a note explaining that "75
#   is only right while NIFTY's is 75" — and NIFTY's lot moved to 65, leaving a gross cap of 300
#   against a real four-leg lot of 260. The envelope was 15% wider than the "exactly one lot" the
#   profile claims to enforce, and nothing said so.
#
#   So do not trust the numbers below either. Read the CURRENT lot size off the Box row, or from
#   the startup diagnostic that compares the selected underlying's lot size against these two caps
#   and refuses a live boot when this profile's exact-one-lot claim is not actually true.
#
# If you trade anything other than NIFTY, set all four from YOUR instrument:
#     BOX_LIVE_MAX_OPEN_LEG_QUANTITY       = its one-lot quantity (read it off the Box row)
#     BOX_LIVE_MAX_GROSS_OPEN_LEG_QUANTITY = 4 x that
#     BOX_LIVE_MAX_BOX_CAPITAL_RUPEES      = above the box's GROSS ORDER NOTIONAL, not margin
#     BOX_LIVE_RECOVERY_RESERVE_RUPEES     = ~25% of that, held back for an unwind
#
# NIFTY 65 → 65 / 260.  PAYTM 725 → 725 / 2900. Do not raise the per-leg cap globally "to be
# safe" — it is the barrier that stops a large stock lot being half-executed.
BOX_LIVE_MAX_OPEN_LEG_QUANTITY=65
BOX_LIVE_MAX_GROSS_OPEN_LEG_QUANTITY=260
# Asserts that the two ceilings above describe EXACTLY ONE LOT across the four legs of one Box.
# With this set, a live boot is REFUSED unless gross == 4 x per-leg, which is what catches an
# exchange lot-size change applied to one of the two and not the other — the 75→65 move left this
# file at 65/300 for exactly that reason. Unset it if you deliberately want a wider envelope than
# one lot; do not widen the per-leg cap to make the arithmetic agree.
BOX_LIVE_EXACT_ONE_LOT=true
BOX_LIVE_MAX_BOX_CAPITAL_RUPEES=100000
BOX_LIVE_RECOVERY_RESERVE_RUPEES=25000


# ═══ §13. FUNDING ADMISSION — ALL ARMED ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# With these on, MISSING or STALE broker evidence REFUSES the entry rather than assuming.
#
# ⚠ ALL THREE DEFAULT TO false, AND ALL THREE OFF IN LIVE NOW BLOCKS NEW ENTRY. With none of them
#   set, economic admission returns before reading anything — a real-money entry would be admitted
#   with no funds or margin evidence at all. That state used to be merely *reported* as
#   `checks_disabled` in a diagnostics field; it now raises the entry blocker
#   `funding_checks_disabled`, visible in operational_readiness.entry.reasons.
#
#   It blocks NEW ENTRY only. Exits, protective cancels, emergency residual flattening and
#   reconciliation are unaffected, which is also why this is a readiness blocker rather than a boot
#   refusal — a process that will not start cannot unwind a position the broker is still holding.
#
#   BOX_LIVE_REQUIRE_STAGE_FUNDING=true implies the funds-cover and margin-evidence checks, so it
#   alone is sufficient to clear the blocker. All three are set here for explicitness.
BOX_LIVE_REQUIRE_FUNDS_COVER=true
BOX_LIVE_REQUIRE_MARGIN_EVIDENCE=true
BOX_LIVE_REQUIRE_STAGE_FUNDING=true
BOX_LIVE_FUNDS_FRESHNESS_MAX_AGE_MS=5000
BOX_LIVE_MARGIN_FRESHNESS_MAX_AGE_MS=5000
BOX_LIVE_EVIDENCE_READ_TIMEOUT_MS=2500
BOX_LIVE_EVIDENCE_CONCURRENT_READS=false


# ═══ §14. FRESHNESS AND FOUR-LEG COHERENCE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# THESE VALUES DO NOT NEED CHANGING FOR A HOST FURTHER FROM THE EXCHANGE, and it is worth knowing
# why, because the intuition points the other way.
#
# The cross-leg gates bound DISPERSION BETWEEN THE FOUR LEGS, not absolute latency. All four books
# arrive over the SAME websocket connection, so a network path that is uniformly N ms longer shifts
# all four arrival times equally and leaves the spread between them unchanged. A more distant host
# does not make the four legs disagree with each other more.
#
# The one gate that does compare against absolute time is BOX_MAX_RECEIVE_TO_EXCHANGE_DELAY_MS,
# and at 5000 ms against a path difference of single-digit milliseconds it has vast headroom. Same
# for the two age limits.
#
# What genuinely does grow with distance is total four-leg completion time — already p50 ~1.6 s in
# the simulated benchmark, so a few milliseconds is noise at that scale. This was never a
# millisecond-scale strategy; the general ceiling of 15000 ms in `.env.example` says so outright.
#
# ⚠ WHAT DOES MATTER ON A NEW HOST IS THE CLOCK, not the distance. `maxExchangeAheadOfReceiveMs`
#   treats an exchange stamp too far ahead of local receive time as a clock fault, so host clock
#   skew can cause false coherence rejections that look like market conditions. Configure the
#   platform's own time source — on Azure that is the host-provided PTP/VMICTimeSync via chrony,
#   NOT the AWS 169.254.169.123 endpoint that docs/MUMBAI_EC2_PROFILE.md prescribes. Verify with
#   `chronyc tracking` and confirm the offset is well inside a millisecond before a live run.
# ⚠ TIGHTENED FOR THE SUPERVISED TRIAL: the general default is 15000 ms, which is right for the
#   system at large and far too loose for a first real-money run. A four-leg box is priced off all
#   four books at once, and a 15-second-old quote is not a price — it is a memory. At that age the
#   edge the scanner computed can have been gone for most of the interval, and the entry would be
#   taken against arithmetic nobody could still verify.
#
#   3000 ms is chosen against the measured shape of the strategy rather than as a round number:
#   four-leg completion is p50 ~1.6 s, so 3 s still admits a quote that is fresh relative to the
#   time the entry itself takes, while refusing one that predates the decision. Tighter than ~2 s
#   starts refusing healthy entries on ordinary NIFTY option quiet periods, where a strike can
#   simply not print for a second or two.
#
#   This is the INDIVIDUAL quote age and nothing else. Four separate, deliberately distinct
#   concepts follow it and NONE of them is changed here: global feed age, cross-leg RECEIVE
#   dispersion, cross-leg EXCHANGE dispersion, and receive-vs-exchange delay. In particular do not
#   "tighten" the exchange bound to match — read the note below it first.
BOX_QUOTE_MAX_AGE_MS=3000
BOX_FEED_MAX_AGE_MS=5000
BOX_MAX_CROSS_LEG_RECEIVE_DISPERSION_MS=500
# ⚠ CORRECTED. This previously read 250 with a comment claiming that, because 250 ms is
#   unsatisfiable from Kite's whole-second stamps, "the RECEIVE-time gate above is the one that
#   actually governs". THAT WAS WRONG, and it cost a full paper session: `evaluateBookCoherence`
#   applies the exchange bound whenever it is > 0 AND all four legs carry a stamp — which for Kite
#   depth packets is ALWAYS. So 250 did not fall back to receive-time; it became a hair trigger.
#
#   Kite stamps books in epoch SECONDS (src/ticker.ts: exchangeTs = exSec * 1000), so cross-leg
#   dispersion can only ever be 0, 1000, 2000 … ms. Four books published 160 ms apart measure 0 ms
#   if they share a second and 1000 ms if they straddle the boundary — and 1000 > 250 was refused as
#   cross_leg_time_skew. Observed effect: 100% of entries refused on a healthy 200-underlying feed.
#
#   1000 keeps this as a COARSE sanity check that still catches genuinely separated stamps (e.g.
#   15s/18s/15s/19s = 4000 ms, still refused). Sub-second coherence is enforced by the RECEIVE-time
#   gate above, which uses our own millisecond arrival clock and is the precise constraint. Setting
#   this below 1000 buys no tightness — only dependence on where a second boundary fell.
#   0 disables it entirely and leaves receive-time as the sole gate.
BOX_MAX_CROSS_LEG_EXCHANGE_DISPERSION_MS=1000
BOX_MAX_RECEIVE_TO_EXCHANGE_DELAY_MS=5000
BOX_COHERENCE_ZERO_DISPERSION_DISABLES_IN_LIVE=false


# ═══ §15. ENTRY ECONOMICS — what a box must clear to be taken ════════════════════════════════
# THE gate: expected NET profit after every cost. The safety buffer is deducted INSIDE this
# figure, so raising the buffer makes the gate strictly harder to clear — it is part of the
# decision, not merely a reported number.
BOX_MIN_EXPECTED_NET_PROFIT=1200
BOX_SAFETY_BUFFER=150

# Deducted from the projection at DETECTION, then MEASURED and recorded at execution rather than
# deducted twice. Keep both — a projection with no slippage allowance admits boxes that cannot
# survive their own fill.
BOX_EXPECTED_ENTRY_SLIPPAGE=250
BOX_EXPECTED_EXIT_SLIPPAGE=250

# Never admit an entry whose charges could not be priced. Leave true: an unpriced charge means the
# net-profit gate is being evaluated against an unknown, which is not a gate.
BOX_REQUIRE_PRICED_CHARGES=true

# Stop trading the box out before expiry rather than carrying it into the close. 45 minutes.
BOX_EXPIRY_SAFETY_MINUTES=45


# ═══ §15b. CIRCUIT BREAKERS — set explicitly, not left to defaults ═══════════════════════════
# These are the limits that STOP trading after something has already gone wrong. All three trip a
# STICKY breaker: it disarms entry and stays tripped until the process restarts, which is the
# correct behaviour for a supervised run — you want to look at what happened, not have it retry.
#
# ⚠ The daily loss limit is in RUPEES and compares against realised P&L, so it only bites after a
#   loss is BOOKED. On a single-box trial one box cannot lose more than the box can lose, so treat
#   this as a backstop against a repeated-attempt scenario rather than as position sizing. Set it
#   to a number you are genuinely willing to lose today.
BOX_LIVE_DAILY_LOSS_LIMIT=5000

# Broker rejects before the breaker trips. A reject usually means the order was malformed or the
# account cannot support it — three of those is a reason to stop and look, not to keep trying.
BOX_LIVE_REJECT_LIMIT=3

# Consecutive technical failures (timeouts, transport errors) before the breaker trips.
BOX_LIVE_CONSECUTIVE_FAILURE_LIMIT=3


# ═══ §16. ORDER STREAM ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Off for a first supervised run: REST reconciliation is the proven path, and the stream adds a
# second moving part on the day you least want one.
ZERODHA_ORDER_STREAM_ENABLED=false


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# §17. ON THE DAY — every step is a separate, deliberate action
#
#   The login must come BEFORE the live boot, not after. See §8: a live-mode boot throws when no
#   authenticated Zerodha session exists, so the old ordering (boot live, then log in) cannot work
#   and produces a BOOT_FAILURE crash-loop.
#
#   PHASE 1 — PAPER, to get a token and prove the configuration
#   1. Apply migrations. Confirm 012 applied — the boot log prints `[PG] migrations: N applied,
#      M already present`; 012 is in when the total reaches 12.
#   2. Set §8 to the PAPER posture (paper_legging / false / false). Start the backend.
#   3. Run:  node dist/box/effectiveConfig.js   and read back the four LOOSER-on-typo variables
#      listed at the top of this file.
#   4. Log in to Zerodha through the UI. Confirm the account is the one you expect. The token is
#      now encrypted in PostgreSQL and survives a restart.
#   5. Optional but cheap: let the paper session run. It exercises arming, the blocklist and the
#      inventory ceiling with nothing at risk. It does NOT exercise any BOX_LIVE_* ceiling —
#      those live in the order manager, which paper never constructs.
#
#   PHASE 2 — PROMOTE TO LIVE
#   6. Set §8 to the LIVE posture (live / true / true). Restart.
#   7. On /api/box/status confirm:
#        execution_mode "live" · db_enabled true · reconciliation_complete true
#        unknown_orders 0 · excluded_underlyings.readable true
#        risk.max_open_boxes_all_modes 1 · risk.box_inventory_held 0
#        operational_readiness.blockers — clear EVERY entry-scoped one
#   8. Set the blocklist for the day.
#   9. Arm in THIS order, so the panic button is live before anything can be sent:
#        live orders → emergency flatten → entry
#  10. Arm the session explicitly and CHECK the response:
#        POST /api/box/session/arm { "max_completed_trades": 1, "max_entry_attempts": 1 }
#      Confirm it reports max_entry_attempts 1 and remaining_entry_attempts 1. Both ceilings are
#      SNAPSHOTTED at arm time — editing this file and restarting does NOT rebind a session that
#      is already armed. This is the likeliest way to believe a bound is on while it is off.
#  11. RUN the scanner.
#
# ⚠ HAVE THESE OPEN IN A TERMINAL BEFORE YOU ARM. If the websocket drops while the box is open,
#   NO EXIT IS ATTEMPTED — the monitor requires a healthy feed, there is no REST fallback on the
#   exit path, and expiry-safety is checked after the same gate. Getting flat with a dead feed is
#   an operator action:
#        POST /api/box/live/cancel-working
#        POST /api/box/live/flatten
#   Both are independent of the entry controls, and reduction does NOT depend on the broker
#   account being nameable — verified, and covered by tests D2/P0-4/A3.
#
# §18. AFTER IT GOES FLAT — nothing stops by itself in this build:
#        POST /api/box/stop
#        POST /api/box/session/disarm
#        GET  /api/box/trades/history?scope=today     the numbers
#        GET  /api/box/events?limit=200               the decision trail
#        GET  /api/box/execution-attempts             anything attempted and aborted
#   `exit_charge_reconciliation` starts `pending`; fees read immediately after flat are LOCAL
#   estimates. Wait for it to settle before treating them as final.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
